-1759-def triangle_findings(tcs, llrs):\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1760-    \"\"\"SR/LLR citation coherence. A TC may cite an\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1761-    SR and an LLR together so one test discharges both the \"SR needs a TC\" and\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1762-    \"LLR needs a TC\" rules; the SR<->LLR relationship itself is recorded\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1763-    canonically on the LLR's SR-Refs. This keeps the derived citation honest: when\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1764-    a TC cites both an SR and an LLR, each cited LLR must descend from one of the\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1765-    SRs the same TC cites (its SR-Refs must intersect them). An incoherent pairing\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1766-    (a TC citing LLR-1 next to SR-2 when LLR-1 decomposes SR-1) is wrong at any\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1767-    stage, so it joins the integrity floor, not the gate-scoped orphan set. A TC\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1768-    that cites only LLRs (no SR) has no SR to contradict — the orphan rules cover\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1769-    it. An LLR with no SR-Refs is already an orphan, so it is not double-reported.\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1770-\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1771-    Implements: SR-157, LLR-002\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1772-    \"\"\"\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1773-    llr_parents = {\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1774-        r[\"LLR-ID\"]: set(refs(r.get(\"SR-Refs\"))) for r in llrs if r.get(\"LLR-ID\")\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1775-    }\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1776-    out = []\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1777-    for r in tcs:\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1778-        tid = r.get(\"TC-ID\")\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1779-        if not tid:\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1780-            continue\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1781-        cited = refs(r.get(\"Verifies\"))\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1782-        cited_srs = {x for x in cited if ID_PATTERNS[\"SR\"].match(x)}\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1783-        if not cited_srs:\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1784-            continue\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1785-        for x in cited:\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1786-            parents = llr_parents.get(x)\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1787-            if parents and not (parents & cited_srs):\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1788-                out.append(\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1789-                    \"TC {} cites {} alongside SR {} but {} decomposes {} (its \"\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1790-                    \"SR-Refs) — an SR/LLR pair in one TC must share the parent \"\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1791-                    \"link recorded on the LLR\".format(\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1792-                        tid,\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1793-                        x,\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1794-                        \", \".join(sorted(cited_srs)),\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1795-                        x,\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1796-                        \", \".join(sorted(parents)),\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1797-                    )\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1798-                )\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1799-    return out\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1800-\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1801-\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1802-# The module-path normalizer MOVED TO trace_text.py at re-tier v2 S5 (WI-464),\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1803-# unchanged: `module_endpoints` and the endpoint classifier there read the same\nproject-trajectory/scripts/trace.py-1804-# two naming conventions this module's seam rules do, so one home there beat a\nproject